## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of conditional Terraform blocks, ensuring safe incremental changes and plan stability when toggling a feature flag.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic blocks in Terraform
- Conditional resource configuration
- AWS S3 server-side encryption basics
- Plan drift and backward compatibility

## Code Example
```javascript
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "b" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  dynamic "server_side_encryption_configuration" {
    for_each = var.enable_sse ? [1] : []
    content {
      rule {
        apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
          sse_algorithm = "AES256"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support SSE with a customer-managed key (KMS) via kms_master_key_id?
- What tests would you add to validate idempotency when toggling the flag across environments?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable drift-resilient Terraform workflow across multiple AWS accounts while ensuring safe automation and auditable infrastructure changes.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection, automatic remediation, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA)
- Environment-specific remote backends, provider aliasing, central baseline state
- Module architecture boundaries, testing frameworks (Kitchen-Terraform/Terratest), CI/CD gates

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?
